# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
register_gate "tests/test_base_retarget.py" "BASE-RETARGET GATE: PASS" "PR retargeted onto main is analyzed, not silently missed (audit r5 silent-miss)" "BASE-RETARGET: a PR retargeted ONTO the protected branch must be analyzed, not silently missed. The audited silent-miss: GitHub fires only pull_request.edited (with changes.base) on a base change -- never synchronize -- so an 'edited' that was not an analyze action left a PR (re)heading to main with NO lanes + NO check; worse, open-on-main -> retarget OFF (tombstoned via release_change_on_main) -> retarget BACK ON main stayed PERMANENTLY silenced (concluded-guard). Fix: an 'edited' carrying changes.base (base==default) is analyzed like an open (should_analyze) and bypasses the concluded-guard (action=='edited' is not in the guarded set) so it re-activates. Proven on the REAL handle_event + FakeGitHub + real DB: (1) a never-seen PR retargeted onto main IS analyzed (a check posts, result not noop/skip); (2) no over-trigger -- a plain title/body edit (no changes.base) stays a clean noop; (3) off->back round-trip re-activates (open-on-main -> edited-off -> edited-back is analyzed again, not permanently tombstoned)" "base-retarget onto main is analyzed (check posted), a plain edit does not over-trigger, and the off->back round-trip re-activates instead of staying tombstoned" 24
